    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "VictoriaMetrics"
      },
      "description": "The two halves of a memtable's memory footprint, per table per node. On-heap holds the index structures and, by default, the partition keys; off-heap holds the cell data when memtable_allocation_type is offheap_objects or offheap_buffers. The split matters because only the on-heap half competes with the JVM heap and can drive GC pressure: a table that is large off-heap and small on-heap is cheap, the reverse is not. Both halves count toward the flush threshold. OFF-HEAP READS ZERO ON A DEFAULT CONFIG: memtable_allocation_type is heap_buffers unless it is changed, which puts every byte on the heap. Filtered to $keyspace so the ~57 system tables do not drown the workload.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "color": {
            "mode": "palette-classic"
          },
          "custom": {
            "axisBorderShow": false,
